WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.640
In the earlier videos from this week, as well as from the videos from the past several weeks,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.440
you've already seen the basic building blocks of forward propagation and back propagation,

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.940
the key components you need to implement a deep neural network.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:17.940
Let's see how you can put these components together to build your deep net.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.340
Here's the network with a few layers.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:27.140
Let's pick one layer and look at the computations focusing on just that layer for now.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:36.140
So for layer L, you have some parameters wL and bL, and for the forward prop,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:48.740
you will input the activations aL-1 from the previous layer and output aL.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:01:00.540
So the way we did this previously was you compute zL equals wL times aL-1 plus bL,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:07.940
and then aL equals g of zL.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.940
So that's how you go from the input aL-1 to the output aL.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:20.740
And it turns out that for later use, it will be useful to also cache the value zL.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:28.140
So let me include this cache as well because storing the value zL will be useful

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.740
for the back propagation step later.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.340
And then for the backward step, for the back propagation step,

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.740
again focusing on the computation for this layer L,

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:51.720
you're going to implement a function that inputs dA of L and outputs dA L-1.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:59.120
And just to flesh out the details, the input is actually dA of L as well as the cache.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.120
So you have available to you the value of zL that you computed.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.920
And then in addition to outputting dA of minus 1,

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:14.120
you will output the gradients you want in order to implement gradient descent for learning.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:20.120
So this is the basic structure of how you implement this forward step.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.720
We're going to call the forward function as well as this backward step,

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.020
which we're going to call a backward function.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.620
So just to summarize, in layer L, you're going to have the forward step

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.020
or the forward proper, the forward function, input aL minus 1,

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.520
and output aL.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.820
And in order to make this computation, you need to use wL and bl.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:52.020
And it also outputs a cache which contains zL.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:03:00.220
And then the backward function using the backprob step will be another function

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:08.320
that now inputs dA of L and outputs dA L-1.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.820
So it tells you, given the derivatives with respect to these activations,

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:16.820
that's dA of L, what are the derivatives?

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:20.420
How much do I wish, you know, aL minus 1 changes,

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:25.320
to compute the derivatives with respect to the activations from the previous layer?

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.720
Within this box, you need to use wL and bl.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:34.920
And it turns out along the way you end up computing dzL.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:43.820
And then this box, this backward function, can also output dwL and dbl.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:47.420
And I was sometimes using red arrows to denote the backward iterations

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:51.620
so we prefer we could draw these arrows in red.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.420
So if you can implement these two functions,

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.620
then the basic computation of the neural network will be as follows.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:04.920
You're going to take the input features a0, feed that in,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.920
and that will compute the activations of the first layer, that's all that a1.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:15.120
And to do that, you need w1 and b1.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:21.520
And then we'll also, you know, cache away z1.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.420
Now having done that, you feed that to the second layer,

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:35.020
and then using w2 and b2, you're going to compute the activations of the next layer, a2, and so on.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:43.520
Until eventually, you end up outputting aL, which is equal to y hat.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:52.660
And along the way, we cached all of these values z.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.360
So that's the forward propagation step.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:05.260
Now for the back propagation step, what we're going to do will be a backward sequence of iterations,

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:12.500
in which you are going backwards and computing gradients like so.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.700
So we're actually going to feed in here dA of L,

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:23.000
and then this box will give us dA of L minus 1,

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:32.100
and so on until we get dA2, dA1.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.100
You could actually get one more output to compute dA0,

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:40.300
but this is derivative with respect to input features, which is not useful,

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:46.300
at least for training the weights of these supervised neural networks.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.400
So you could just stop it there.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:55.700
Along the way, backprop also ends up outputting dWL, dBL,

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:00.400
this used parameters wL and bL.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:10.880
This would output dW3, dB3, and so on.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.890
So you end up computing all the derivatives you need.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:22.090
And so just to maybe fill in the structure of this a little bit more,

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:26.090
these boxes will use those parameters as well.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:37.390
WL, bL, and it turns out that we'll see later that inside these boxes will end up computing dZs as well.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:42.490
So one iteration of training for a neural network involves starting with a0,

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:48.390
which is x, and going through forward prop as follows, computing y hat,

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:56.390
and then using that to compute this, and then backprop, doing that.

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:59.990
And now you have all these derivative terms.

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:08.790
And so w will get updated as w minus the learning rate times dW for each of the layers,

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:13.890
and similarly for b.

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:17.790
Now that you've computed backprop and have all these derivatives.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:21.990
So that's one iteration of gradient descent for your neural network.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:25.490
Now before moving on, just one more implementation detail.

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:34.190
Conceptually, it'll be useful to think of the cache here as storing the value of z for the backward functions.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:37.290
But when you implement this, you see this in the programming exercise.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:44.190
When you implement this, you find that the cache may be a convenient way to get this value of the parameters of w1, b1

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.190
into the backward function as well.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:53.090
So in the programming exercise, you actually store the cache z as well as w and b.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:57.590
So let's just store z2, w2, b2.

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:06.890
But from an implementation standpoint, I just find this a convenient way to just get the parameters copied to where you need to use them later

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:08.890
when you're computing backprop.

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:15.490
So that's just an implementation detail that you see when you do the programming exercise.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:19.890
So you've now seen one of the basic building blocks for implementing a deep neural network.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:24.190
In each layer, there's a forward propagation step and there's a corresponding backward propagation step

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:27.790
and there's a cache to pass information from one to the other.

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:32.090
In the next video, we'll talk about how you can actually implement these building blocks.

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:33.590
Let's go on to the next video.
